{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicNormalization","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicNormalization","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicGeodesicZAxis","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicIsometryInversion","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicIsometryAffine"],"declarations":15,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.exists_normalization","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.exists_normalization","kind":"theorem","line":464,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/HyperbolicNormalization.lean#L464-L483","doc":"**WALL-2 — full two-point normalization to the z-axis (DERIVED, sorry-free).**\nFor ANY `x, y ∈ H³` there is a `dH`-isometry `Φ` carrying both `Φ x` and `Φ y`\nonto the z-axis.  The shared-horizontal case reuses the single translation of\n`exists_normalization_vertical`; the distinct-horizontal case is the boundary-\ninversion construction of `exists_normalization_general`.  This is the geometric\nreduction that carries geodesic uniqueness between two arbitrary points down to\nthe already-derived z-axis case (`dH_zpt`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.exists_normalization_isom","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.exists_normalization_isom","kind":"theorem","line":435,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/HyperbolicNormalization.lean#L435-L453","doc":"**WALL-2 — full two-point normalization, INVERTIBLE form (DERIVED,\nsorry-free).**  For ANY `x, y ∈ H³` there is a `dH`-isometry `Φ` with a\n`dH`-isometry left inverse `Ψ` carrying both onto the z-axis.  The invertible\nform; `exists_normalization` is its plain projection.  This is the interface the\nWALL-5 minimiser-uniqueness reduction consumes. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.exists_normalization_vertical_isom","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.exists_normalization_vertical_isom","kind":"theorem","line":400,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/HyperbolicNormalization.lean#L400-L433","doc":"**WALL-2 — vertical (shared-horizontal) normalization, INVERTIBLE form\n(DERIVED, sorry-free).**  As `exists_normalization_vertical`, but also exposing the\n`dH`-isometry left inverse `Ψ = (· − b)` of the single horizontal translation\n`Φ = (· + b)`, `b = zpt x₂ − x`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.exists_normalization_general","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.exists_normalization_general","kind":"theorem","line":391,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/HyperbolicNormalization.lean#L391-L398","doc":"**WALL-2 — general (distinct-horizontal) two-point normalization (DERIVED,\nsorry-free).**  The plain existence form, projecting the normalising map out of\n`exists_normalization_general_isom`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.exists_normalization_general_isom","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.exists_normalization_general_isom","kind":"theorem","line":269,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/HyperbolicNormalization.lean#L269-L389","doc":"**WALL-2 — general (distinct-horizontal) normalization, INVERTIBLE form\n(DERIVED, sorry-free).**  Strengthens `exists_normalization_general` by also\nproducing a `dH`-isometry LEFT INVERSE `Ψ` of the normalising map `Φ`\n(`Ψ = T₁⁻¹ ∘ T₂ ∘ T₃⁻¹`, using that the boundary inversion `T₂` is involutive).\n`Ψ` witnesses that `Φ` is injective and preserves `dH` exactly\n(`dH_eq_of_leftInverse`), which is what the WALL-5 minimiser-uniqueness reduction\nneeds. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.invImage_horiz_collapse","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.invImage_horiz_collapse","kind":"theorem","line":211,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/HyperbolicNormalization.lean#L211-L247","doc":"**Boundary-inversion horizontal collapse.**  Let `u` be a unit horizontal\nvector (`u₂ = 0`, `u₀²+u₁² = 1`), `R, ρ > 0`, and let `p` have horizontal part\n`α·u` (i.e. `p₀ = α·u₀`, `p₁ = α·u₁`), height `β`, and lie on the sphere centred at\n`O = c·u` of radius `R`.  Then the boundary inversion at `ξ = (c−R)·u` sends both\nhorizontal coordinates of `p` to `K·uⱼ` with `K = (c−R) + ρ²/(2R)`, a value\nindependent of `p`.  (`p ≠ ξ` — automatic for an interior point, whose height is\npositive while `ξ` is on the boundary.) ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.inversion_coord","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.inversion_coord","kind":"theorem","line":185,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/HyperbolicNormalization.lean#L185-L195","doc":"**The `j`-th coordinate of a boundary inversion.**  `EuclideanGeometry.inversion\nc R p = (R/dist p c)²·(p − c) + c`, so coordinatewise\n`(inversion c R p)ⱼ = (R/dist p c)²·(pⱼ − cⱼ) + cⱼ`.  Same extraction as\n`boundaryInversionIsom.hheight`, but for a general coordinate `j` rather than the\nheight. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.distSq_coords","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.distSq_coords","kind":"theorem","line":176,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/HyperbolicNormalization.lean#L176-L183","doc":"**Pythagorean coordinate expansion of the squared Euclidean distance** on\n`H³`'s carrier: `dist p q² = (p₀−q₀)² + (p₁−q₁)² + (p₂−q₂)²`.  This turns a\nEuclidean-sphere membership `‖p − O‖ = R` into a polynomial identity in the\ncoordinates, which is what the inversion-image computation consumes. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.exists_normalization_vertical","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.exists_normalization_vertical","kind":"theorem","line":132,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/HyperbolicNormalization.lean#L132-L162","doc":"**WALL-2 — vertical (shared-horizontal) two-point normalization (DERIVED,\nsorry-free).**  If `x, y ∈ H³` have equal horizontal coordinates (`x₀ = y₀`,\n`x₁ = y₁`), a single `dH`-isometry (the horizontal translation by `zpt x₂ − x`)\ncarries both onto the z-axis. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.zpt_mem_zAxis","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.zpt_mem_zAxis","kind":"theorem","line":116,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/HyperbolicNormalization.lean#L116-L119","doc":"**`zpt a` is on the z-axis** for `a > 0`: its horizontal coordinates vanish and\nits height is `a > 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.zpt_coord1","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.zpt_coord1","kind":"theorem","line":112,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/HyperbolicNormalization.lean#L112-L114","doc":"The `1`-coordinate of a z-axis point `zpt a = (0,0,a)` vanishes. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.zpt_coord0","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.zpt_coord0","kind":"theorem","line":108,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/HyperbolicNormalization.lean#L108-L110","doc":"The `0`-coordinate of a z-axis point `zpt a = (0,0,a)` vanishes. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.mem_zAxis","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.mem_zAxis","kind":"theorem","line":106,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/HyperbolicNormalization.lean#L106-L106","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.zAxis","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.zAxis","kind":"def","line":101,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/HyperbolicNormalization.lean#L101-L104","doc":"**The z-axis of `H³`** — the vertical geodesic `{(0,0,z) : z > 0}`, the target\nof the normalization.  A point is on it iff both horizontal coordinates vanish and\nthe height is positive. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicMetric.IsDHIsometry.comp","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicMetric.IsDHIsometry.comp","kind":"def","line":71,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/HyperbolicNormalization.lean#L71-L90","doc":"**Composition of `dH`-isometries is a `dH`-isometry.**  If `ψ` and `φ` are\neach conformal-with-the-same-factor-on-the-height, so is `ψ ∘ φ`: its Fréchet\nderivative is the composite `Dψ(φ p) ∘ Dφ(p)` (chain rule), its conformal factor\nis the PRODUCT `lam_ψ(φ p) · lam_φ(p)` (norms scale by the product), and its\nheight scales by the SAME product (`hheight` composed).  So the line element\n`‖v‖/z` — preserved pointwise by each factor — is preserved by the composite.\nThis lets a finite composition of the WALL-1 generators and a boundary inversion\nbe handled as a single `IsDHIsometry`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}